the island of hispaniola here has had a  long history of unification and division  it was a series of individual states  then ruled by the spanish then it was  split with a french ruled entirely by  the french partially ruled by the  spanish and independent haitians  entirely ruled by the haitians divided  between the haitians and the haitians  followed by the independent dominicans  and the spanish again back to  independence and then to shake things up  it was occupied by the americans before  again settling down into the two modern  states of haiti in the dominican  republic which raises the question why  couldn't the island stay united so when  europeans first turned up to the island  it was divided into a number of  chiefdoms which the spanish then  promptly conquered afterwards the entire  island remained under spanish rule for  130 years when france won the eastern  third of it and france's portion of the  island would grow rapidly in wealth  until the french revolutionary wars this  was when france having defeated most of  europe won the rest of hispaniola from  spain there was soon a slave revolt in  the western third napoleon sent troops  to crush it and when that failed the  island was split again this time into  the newly independent haitian empire  with the french in the east which lasted  for a whopping two years before its  emperor jacques got himself unalived and  the country fell into civil war the two  stayed divided until 1820 when the  northern king died and the southern  republic led by jean-pierre boye  reconquered the rest by this time the  french had been kicked out of the  eastern portion of the island with the  spanish back in power and after this  there was a long-standing period of  peace and rebuilding and just kidding  the haitians invaded and bowie declared  himself dictator for life boyer ruled  over all of hispaniola for the next 20  years he paid in indemnity to france in  return for international recognition of  haiti's independence and he kicked out  all of the white landowners from the  east of the island except for the polish  that is they were cool boyer did very  little to unify the two parts of the  island culturally and many in the east  saw themselves as a wholly distinct  people from the haitians who now ruled  them and thus it was time for a calm  discussion by which i mean armed  revolution boyer fled in 1843 and in  1844 the dominican republic declared its  independence the two would remain  separate thereafter with haiti briefly  becoming an empire again and the  dominican republic falling to a spanish  invasion in 1861 this was because the  united states monroe doctrine which  forbid europeans from retaking their old  colonies was somewhat harder to enforce  at this time it didn't matter though  because the dominicans soon revolted and  kicked the spanish out and both parts of  the islands remained as independent  republics thereafter that was until the  early 20th century haiti was still  paying off its debts to france from the  napoleonic era and combined with  expensive wars and general mismanagement  the country had become quite poor  haiti's issues meant that it couldn't  repay some of his debts to an american  bank which meant that it was time for  intervention the united states invaded  the country in 1915 to force through  debt repayment and protect american  interests there the next year the u.s  friendly dominican government was  overthrown and since u.s marines were in  the area in they went both nations were  thus occupied and run by the same  country until the u.s left the dominican  republic in 1924 and haiti in 1934 and  thereafter both haitian and dominican  independence was restored and hispaniola  would remain divided i hope you enjoyed  this episode and a special thanks to my  patrons james bissonette kelly money  maker skye chappelle caution wolf jerry  lamdin jordan longley adam stalter  marcus arsenault wyan hockey spencer  lightfoot rod d martin words abou